"rilke": [
"Entrance. Whoever you are: at evening step out of your room, where you know everything;",
"your house is the last one before the distance: whoever you are.",
"With your eyes, which in their weariness can barely free themselves from the worn threshold,",
"you slowly lift one black tree and set it against the sky: slender, alone.",
"And you have made the world. And it is vast, and like a word that is still ripening in silence.",
"And as your will takes hold of its meaning, tenderly your eyes let it go.",
"Autumn Day. Lord: it is time. The summer was immense.",
"Lay your shadows on the sundials, and on the meadows let the winds go free.",
"Command the last fruits to be full; give them two more southern days,",
"press them to ripeness, and chase the last sweetness into the heavy wine.",
"Whoever has no house now will not build one.",
"Whoever is alone now will stay alone a long time,",
"will wake, and read, and write long letters,",
"and wander the avenues up and down, restless, while the leaves are blowing.",
"Autumn. The leaves are falling, falling as if from far away,",
"as if far gardens withered in the heavens; they fall with gestures that refuse.",
"And in the nights the heavy earth falls from all the stars into loneliness.",
"We all are falling. This hand here falls.",
"And look at the others: it is in them all.",
"And yet there is One who holds this falling endlessly gently in his hands.",
"Solemn Hour. Whoever weeps now somewhere in the world, weeping for no reason in the world, weeps over me.",
"Whoever laughs now somewhere in the night, laughing for no reason in the night, laughs at me.",
"Whoever walks now somewhere in the world, walking for no reason in the world, walks toward me.",
"Whoever dies now somewhere in the world, dying for no reason in the world, looks at me.",
"Evening. The evening slowly changes into the garments held out for it by a row of ancient trees;",
"you watch, and the lands part from you, one rising heavenward, one that falls,",
"and leave you, belonging wholly to neither,",
"not quite so dark as the house that keeps its silence,",
"not quite so surely pledged to the eternal as what becomes a star each night and climbs;",
"and leave you, beyond all untangling, your life: afraid, and huge, and ripening,",
"so that, now bounded and now reaching past itself, it turns in you by turns to stone and to star.",
"Loneliness. Loneliness is like a rain.",
"It rises from the sea toward the evenings; from plains that are far and remote",
"it climbs into the sky, which always holds it,",
"and only from the sky does it fall upon the city.",
"It rains down in the in-between hours, when all the streets turn toward the morning,",
"and when the bodies that found nothing leave each other, disappointed and sad;",
"and when people who hate each other must sleep together in one bed:",
"then loneliness goes with the rivers.",
"Presentiment. I am like a flag surrounded by distances.",
"I sense the winds that are coming, and must live them, while the things below are not yet stirring:",
"the doors still close gently, and in the chimneys there is silence;",
"the windows do not yet tremble, and the dust still lies heavy.",
"Then I already know the storms, and am stirred like the sea,",
"and spread myself out, and fall into myself, and throw myself off,",
"and am alone in the great storm.",
"Progress. And again my deep life rushes louder, as if it moved now between wider banks.",
"Things grow ever more akin to me, and every picture more deeply gazed into.",
"I feel myself more trusted by the nameless:",
"with my senses, as with birds, I reach into the windy heavens from the oak,",
"and into the broken-off day of the ponds my feeling sinks, as if it stood on fish.",
"People at Night. The nights are not made for the crowd.",
"Night parts you from your neighbour, and you should not go looking for him.",
"And if at night you light your room to look into people's faces,",
"you must consider: whose.",
"People are terribly distorted by the light that drips from their faces,",
"and if they have gathered at night, you see a tottering world heaped up in confusion.",
"The Neighbour. Strange violin, are you following me?",
"In how many distant cities has your lonely night already spoken to mine?",
"Are a hundred playing you? Or only one?",
"Are there in every great city those who, without you, would long since have lost themselves in the rivers?",
"And why does it always reach me? Why am I always the neighbour",
"of those who fearfully force you to sing, and to say:",
"life is heavier than the heaviness of all things.",
"Closing Piece. Death is great.",
"We are his, with laughing mouths.",
"When we think ourselves in the midst of life, he dares to weep in the midst of us."
]
